# Agent shell-script discipline

These scripts run **unattended on a CI runner** with a `GITHUB_TOKEN`,
sometimes with `--dangerously-skip-permissions` for Claude Code. The blast
radius is bounded by the runner being ephemeral, but every shortcut here
becomes a real production hazard.

## Hard rules

### 1. Never use `git add -A` or `git add .`

Both stage the entire working tree, including any untracked file the
runner happened to leave behind (caches, build artifacts, accidental
secrets, OS junk like `.DS_Store`). On a CI runner you cannot trust the
worktree.

**Always** stage from an explicit allowlist of code/doc paths:

```bash
SAFE_ADD_PATHS=(backend frontend frontend-internal docs scripts AGENTS.md README.md CONTRIBUTING.md Makefile)
ADD_ARGS=()
for p in "${SAFE_ADD_PATHS[@]}"; do
  [[ -e "$p" ]] && ADD_ARGS+=("$p")
done
git add -- "${ADD_ARGS[@]}"
```

Exception: never. If you genuinely need to stage a new path, add it to
the allowlist explicitly.

### 2. Use a dedicated bot identity per agent script

Every agent commit MUST set both `user.name` and `user.email` inline so
the commit is unambiguously machine-authored. Use a per-script identity
matching the bot detection regex in `claude-ci-autofix.yml` and
`heal-pr.sh`:

```
^claude-(implement|heal|agent|autofix)@
```

Examples in use:
- `Claude Implement <claude-implement@users.noreply.github.com>`
- `Claude Heal     <claude-heal@users.noreply.github.com>`
- `Claude Autofix  <claude-autofix@users.noreply.github.com>`

Adding a new agent identity? Update the regex in **both** detection
sites (heal-pr.sh and claude-ci-autofix.yml) at the same time.

### 3. Tag every agent commit with a trailer

Every agent commit body must include at least one of:

```
Agent-Implemented: tier-2     # Phase F autopilot wrote this
Agent-Heal: tier-2|tier-3     # heal-pr.sh wrote this
Agent-Autofix: tier-1         # autofix-pr.sh wrote this
```

Plus a `Co-authored-by: claude-agent <bot@nexa.farm>` for `git shortlog`.
The trailers drive retry caps and bot-authored detection.

### 4. Always cap retries via commit-trailer counts

Don't store retry state in temp files or labels. Count existing trailers
on the branch:

```bash
COUNT="$(git log "origin/$BRANCH" --grep='^Agent-Heal:' --oneline | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"
if [[ "$COUNT" -ge "$MAX_RETRIES" ]]; then
  # bail
fi
```

Trailers are durable; temp state isn't. CI runners are ephemeral.

### 5. Bot-authored detection: STRONG signals only

Detect bot PRs from **two anchored signals only**:

1. Committer email regex: `^claude-(implement|heal|agent|autofix)@`
   (read with `git log --format='%cE'`)
2. Anchored `^Agent-Implemented:` trailer
   (read with `git log --format='%B'`)

Do NOT use `git log --grep='claude-agent'`. `git log --grep` ORs multiple
patterns and will false-positive on human commits like `"fix regression
from claude-agent run"`.

### 6. Bail loudly, comment back, exit cleanly

When an agent script declines to push (no-touch zone, scope cap, check
failed), it MUST:

1. Print a `BAIL:` line to stderr the runner can grep.
2. Post a diagnostic comment on the issue or PR explaining why.
3. Exit with a clean numeric code (20 / 30 / 31 / 32 / 40 — see
   `implement-issue.sh` exit-code table for the canonical mapping).

Never `exit 1` from a clean bail — the calling workflow uses the exit
code to choose its Slack copy. Reusing `1` muddies the signal.

### 7. Heredoc safety

Bash heredocs choke on stray apostrophes inside variables interpolated
into the body. When composing prompts, prefer `$(cat <<'EOF' ... EOF)`
(quoted EOF, no interpolation) when the body is static, and
`$(cat <<EOF ... EOF)` (unquoted) only when you need interpolation.
Always run `bash -n script.sh` before committing.

### 8. Never trust the worktree to be clean

At the top of any agent script that mutates files, verify the working
tree is clean:

```bash
if ! git diff --quiet || ! git diff --cached --quiet; then
  echo "Error: working tree is dirty. Commit or stash before running." >&2
  exit 10
fi
```

Or, if the script is meant to be run inside a CI runner (where the
checkout is always fresh), document that assumption in the script header.

### 9. PATH safety

Every agent script must export an explicit PATH at the top so it works
both on the user's machine and on the GitHub runner without depending on
shell init files:

```bash
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:$PATH"
```

### 10. `--dangerously-skip-permissions` requires bounded blast radius

Only acceptable when ALL of the following are true:

- runner is ephemeral (GitHub Actions, fresh container)
- working branch was just created from `staging` (the integration branch
  under the staging-first policy; see `AGENTS.md` "Branching policy")
  and is never auto-merged
- result is validated post-edit (no-touch regex, diff-line cap, file-count cap, `make check`)
- output is captured to a log that's surfaced in the workflow run

Violating any of these means you've built an unreviewed direct path
to production. Don't do it.
